=> Explain the flow of try, catch and finally.
    -> The execution starts from the `try` block.
    -> If no exception occurs:
        -> Remaining code inside `try` executes successfully.
        -> `catch` block is skipped.
        -> `finally` block executes.
    -> If an exception occurs:
        -> JVM immediately stops executing the remaining statements inside the `try` block.
        -> JVM searches for the matching `catch` block.
        -> If a matching `catch` is found:
            -> It executes the `catch` block.
            -> Then executes the `finally` block.
        -> If no matching `catch` is found:
            -> `finally` block executes.
            -> Exception is propagated to the caller.
    -> After `finally`, the remaining program continues (if the exception is handled).

=> Will the finally block always execute?
    -> In almost all cases, Yes.
    -> Whether:
        -> Exception occurs.
        -> No exception occurs.
        -> Exception is caught.
        -> There is a return statement.
    -> The `finally` block executes before the method exits.

=> In which situations will finally not execute?
    -> If the JVM terminates abruptly.
        -> Example: `System.exit(0)`
    -> JVM crashes.
    -> Power failure / System shutdown.
    -> Native code forcibly terminates the JVM.
    -> Interview Tip:
        -> Returning from the `try` or `catch` block DOES NOT skip the `finally` block.

=> Difference between Checked and Unchecked Exceptions.
    -> Checked Exception
        -> Checked at compile time.
        -> Compiler forces us to handle or declare them.
        -> Extend the `Exception` class (excluding RuntimeException).
        -> Generally occur due to external resources.
            -> File.
            -> Database.
            -> Network.
    -> Unchecked Exception
        -> Checked at runtime.
        -> Compiler does not force handling.
        -> Extend the `RuntimeException` class.
        -> Usually occur because of programming mistakes.

=> Give examples of Checked Exceptions.
    -> IOException
    -> SQLException
    -> FileNotFoundException
    -> ClassNotFoundException
    -> ParseException
    -> Custom Exception (extends Exception)

=> Give examples of Unchecked Exceptions.
    -> NullPointerException
    -> ArithmeticException
    -> ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException
    -> NumberFormatException
    -> IllegalArgumentException
    -> StackOverflowError (Note: It is an Error, not an Exception.)
    -> Custom Exception (extends RuntimeException)

=> What happens if a method declares `throws IOException`?
    -> It does NOT handle the exception.
    -> It simply declares that the method may throw an IOException.
    -> Responsibility is transferred to the caller.
    -> The caller must:
        -> Handle it using `try-catch`.
        -> Or declare it again using `throws`.
    -> If nobody handles it:
        -> The exception propagates up the call stack.
        -> Eventually reaches the JVM.

=> Difference between throw and throws.
    -> throw
        -> Used to explicitly throw an exception.
        -> Used inside the method body.
        -> Throws one exception at a time.
        -> Followed by an Exception object.
        -> Example:
            -> throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid Age");

    -> throws
        -> Used to declare that a method may throw exceptions.
        -> Used with the method signature.
        -> Can declare multiple exceptions.
        -> Does not throw the exception itself.
        -> Responsibility is passed to the caller.

    -> Interview Tip:
        -> Remember this one-liner:
            -> `throw` = Actually throws the exception.
            -> `throws` = Declares that the method may throw the exception.